is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The control plane is healthy; the data plane on router2 is not. RIP is up and advertising all expected routes: router0, router1, and router2 have R entries for 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16, and ripd is running on router2 (ps shows /usr/lib/frr/ripd, and “show ip route” contains multiple R>* routes). The symptom appears only for sources in 192.168.1.0/24 (IT) and 192.168.2.0/24 (CS), while 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24 work—this matches the firewall on router2: iptables FORWARD has explicit drops for -s 192.168.1.0/24 and -s 192.168.2.0/24 with nonzero counters (41 and 39 packets dropped). Traceroutes from it_lab1 and cs_lab1 reach router2 (20.20.0.2) and then stop, confirming forwarding is blocked at router2. Pings from router0/router1 to 1.0.0.x and 128.168.0.x succeed because their source IPs are not filtered.

Affected component: router2’s firewall (iptables) FORWARD chain. Mechanism: packets sourced from 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 are dropped at router2, preventing access to Server Room (1.0.0.0/8) and Internet Lab (128.168.0.0/16). Localization: router2, iptables FORWARD chain. Root cause: unintended drop rules for the IT and CS subnets were introduced during maintenance.

Corrective fix: remove or precede those DROP entries with explicit ACCEPT rules to 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16 (e.g., iptables -D FORWARD -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j DROP and same for 192.168.2.0/24, or add ACCEPTs above them) and persist the change. Do not restart ripd; it is already running and functioning correctly. === END ===